catalogNbr": "1930", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "SHUM", "catalogNbr": "1930", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/21/2025", "sessionEndDt": "05/17/2025", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "By posing seemingly simple questions such as what is love and who has the right to love, this introductory-level lecture course surveys how love has been experienced and expressed from the pre-modern period to the present. Through case studies of familial and conjugal love in Africa, Asia, the US, Europe, and South and Latin America, the course will examine the debates about and enactment's of what constitutes the appropriate way to show love and affection in different cultures and historical contexts. Among the themes we will explore are questions of sexuality, marriage, kinship, and gender rights. A final unit will examine these themes through modern technologies such as the Internet, scientific advances in medicine, and a growing awareness that who and how we love is anything but simple or universal.", "catalogBreadth": null, "catalogDistr": "(HST-AS, SCD-AS) (D-AG, HA-AG)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "(EC-SAP)", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2895, "crseId": 363820, "crseOfferNbr": 2, "subject": "LGBT", "catalogNbr": "2290", "titleShort": "Intro to LGBT studies", "titleLong": "Introduction to Lesbian, Gay, Bisexual, and Transgender Studies", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "LEC", "ssrComponentLong": "Lecture", "section": "001", "classNbr": 4997, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "11:40AM", "timeEnd": "12:55PM", "startDt": "01/21/2025", "endDt": "05/06/2025", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 4, "netid": "mkm23", "firstName": "Kate", "middleName": "", "lastName": "McCullough"}], "pattern": "TR", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/21/2025", "endDt": "05/17/2025", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["LEC"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "FGSS", "catalogNbr": "2290", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/21/2025", "sessionEndDt": "05/17/2025", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "This course offers an introduction to central issues, debates, and theories that characterize the field of Lesbian, Gay, Bisexual, and Transgender (LGBT) Studies. Starting from the assumption that neither \"sex\" nor \"sexuality\" is a private experience or category, we will explore some of the ways that these powerfully public and political terms have circulated in social, legal, economic, and cultural spheres. We will also examine how these categories are situated in relation to other formative categories including race, ethnicity, religion, family, marriage, reproduction, the economy, and the state. Using a comparative and intersectional approach, we will read from various disciplines to assess the tools that LGBT studies offers for understanding power and culture in our contemporary world.", "catalogBreadth": null, "catalogDistr": "(SCD-AS) (D-AG)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2895, "crseId": 365700, "crseOfferNbr": 4, "subject": "LGBT", "catalogNbr": "2350", "titleShort": "Literature and Medicine", "titleLong": "Literature and Medicine", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "LEC", "ssrComponentLong": "Lecture", "section": "001", "classNbr": 18644, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "10:10AM", "timeEnd": "11:00AM", "startDt": "01/21/2025", "endDt": "05/06/2025", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "ejc244", "firstName": "Elisha", "middleName": "Jane", "lastName": "Cohn"}], "pattern": "MWF", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/21/2025", "endDt": "05/17/2025", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["LEC"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "AMST", "catalogNbr": "2330", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "BSOC", "catalogNbr": "2350", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "ENGL", "catalogNbr": "2350", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "FGSS", "catalogNbr": "2350", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "SHUM", "catalogNbr": "2350", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/21/2025", "sessionEndDt": "05/17/2025", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "How does literary language depict the experience of physical suffering? Can a poem or a novel palliate pain, illness, even the possibility of death? From darkly comic narratives of black plague to the rise and fall of hysteria to depictions of the AIDS crisis, this course examines literature centered on medical practices from the early modern period through the twentieth century. Why have medical practices changed, and how do writers address their political, social, and ideological implications? Readings will include a broad range of genres, including poetry (Dickinson, Whitman, Keats), fiction (McEwan, Chekhov, Gilman, Kafka, Camus), theater (Kushner), nonfiction prose (Woolf, Freud), and critical theory (Foucault, Scarry, Canguilhem, Sontag).", "catalogBreadth": null, "catalogDistr": "(ALC-AS, SCD-AS) (CA-AG, D-AG, LA-AG)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2895, "crseId": 367858, "crseOfferNbr": 3, "subject": "LGBT", "catalogNbr": "3210", "titleShort": "Gender and the Brain", "titleLong": "Gender and the Brain", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "LEC", "ssrComponentLong": "Lecture", "section": "001", "classNbr": 4867, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "02:55PM", "timeEnd": "04:10PM", "startDt": "01/21/2025", "endDt": "05/06/2025", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "sbd3", "firstName": "Shelby", "middleName": "Barbara", "lastName": "Dietz"}], "pattern": "MW", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [{"classNotesSeq": 1, "descrlong": "Prerequisite: BIONB 2220, BIOMG 3320, FGSS 2010, LGBT 2290 or permission of instructor. For Neurobiology and Behavior (NBB) concentration: this course may be used toward the additional 7 credit requirement, but does not qualify as an advanced course. If a student enrolls in a non-College of Agriculture and Life Sciences (CALS) section of a cross-listed course, it will not automatically be counted as CALS credits in their degree progress. However, we can still give them CALS credit for the class, they just need to ask us to code it."}], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/21/2025", "endDt": "05/17/2025", "addConsent": "I", "addConsentDescr": "Instructor Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "C", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["LEC"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "BIONB", "catalogNbr": "3215", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "FGSS", "catalogNbr": "3210", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/21/2025", "sessionEndDt": "05/17/2025", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "Why are boys more likely than girls to be diagnosed with autism, and why are women more likely than men to be diagnosed with depression? Are there different \"gay\" and \"straight\" brains? And how does brain science interact with gender and sexuality in popular debate? Reading and discussing the original scientific papers and related critical texts, we will delve into the neuroscience of gender. In this course, we will delve into the neuroscience of gender difference. Reading the original scientific papers and related critical texts, we will ask whether we can find measurable physical differences in male and female brains, and what these differences might be. Do men and women solve spatial puzzles differently, as measured physiologically? Do nonhuman animals display sex-specific behaviors mediated by brain structure, and can we extrapolate these findings to human behavior? Why are boys three times more likely than girls to be diagnosed as autistic, and is there any connection between the predominantly male phenomenon of autism and other stereotypically male mental traits? Are there physical representations of sexual orientation in the brain, and how are these related to gender identity? And how are scientific studies represented and misrepresented in popular debate?", "catalogBreadth": null, "catalogDistr": "(BIO-AS, SCD-AS) (D-AG, OPHLS-AG)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "Prerequisite: BIONB 2220, BIOMG 3320, FGSS 2010, and LGBT 2290\u00a0or permission of instructor.", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": ["Demonstrate knowledge of the state of the field in gender and neuroscience.", "Be able to apply concepts from history, philosophy, and critical theory to scientific texts.", "Demonstrate understanding of the interaction of experimental design, stereotypes, and preconceptions in how studies are carried out and communicated.", "Be aware of the range of experimental techniques available in neuroscience, and their advantages and limitations."], "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2895, "crseId": 374314, "crseOfferNbr": 4, "subject": "LGBT", "catalogNbr": "3575", "titleShort": "Intro to Black German Studies", "titleLong": "Introduction to Black German Studies", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "SEM", "ssrComponentLong": "Seminar", "section": "101", "classNbr": 20473, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "10:10AM", "timeEnd": "11:25AM", "startDt": "01/21/2025", "endDt": "05/06/2025", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 1, "netid": "ems498", "firstName": "Elizabeth", "middleName": "Mae", "lastName": "Schoppelrei"}], "pattern": "TR", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "01/21/2025", "endDt": "05/17/2025", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "P", "instrModeDescrshort": "In Person", "instrModeDescr": "In Person", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["SEM"], "gradingBasis": "GRD", "gradingBasisShort": "Graded", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter grades only", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "ASRC", "catalogNbr": "3575", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "FGSS", "catalogNbr": "3575", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "GERST", "catalogNbr": "3575", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "1", "sessionBeginDt": "01/21/2025", "sessionEndDt": "05/17/2025", "sessionLong": "Regular Academic Session", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "During her time in Germany, the U.S. poet Audre Lorde (who described herself as \"Black, lesbian, mother, warrior poet\") sought out community and encouraged Black Germans to speak up and make themselves visible as Afro-Germans in a country that often ignored or invalidated their existences. This is one of many crucial moments in Black German history and Black German Studies. In this course will explore the histories, activism, literature, and scholarship that arise from Afro-German communities and have shaped the field of Black German Studies. We will engage with films, novels, poetry, short stories, graphic narratives, music videos, and scholarly essays. Considering the great variety of media and genres, we will spend class time not only developing a shared vocabulary to talk about each text, but we will also learn to apply differing methods of analysis in our class discussions and individual assignments.", "catalogBreadth": null, "catalogDistr": "(ALC-AS, SCD-AS) (CA-AG, LA-AG, D-AG)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": "", "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Spring.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": "", "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": null, "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2895, "crseId": 370485, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "LGBT", "catalogNbr": "3990", "titleShort": "Undergrad Independent Study", "titleLong": "Undergraduate Independent Study", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "IND", "ssrComponentLong": "Independent Study", "section": "601", "classNbr": 5248, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "", "timeEnd": "", "startDt": "01/21/2025", "endDt": "05/06/2025", "instructors": [], "pattern": "", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [{"classNotesSeq": 1, "descrlong": "To apply: first discuss the Independent Study with the course supervisor, and then complete the online Independent Study form at https://data.arts.